\section{Separate Architectural Facts from Estimates}

A machine description answers more than ``what opcode prints here?'' It states which registers and immediates a form accepts, what state it reads and writes, whether any outputs tie to inputs, and how the bits of an instruction are laid out. A selector consumes the semantics and operand restrictions. An assembler uses the encoding and fixups. A scheduler needs dependence and resource information, and a disassembler needs decoding precedence when different syntax aliases share an encoding. Repeating an operand rule independently in each component makes agreement accidental. Store the rule once or make agreement a tested interface.

Himmelbauer and colleagues' \emph{The Vienna Architecture Description Language} separates an instruction set architecture description from a microarchitecture description. This lets tools share architectural semantics while varying the implementation. A different core can change the desirability of a form while leaving its bytes, architectural result, and clobbers intact. Feature requirements belong on the legality side: a favorable latency estimate cannot make an unsupported instruction encodable for a deployment target.

\section{Worked Description: A Signed Immediate}

Take the RISC-V integer \texttt{ADDI} form on RV32I. It reads \texttt{rs1}, adds a sign-extended 12-bit immediate modulo $2^{32}$, and writes \texttt{rd}; a write to register \texttt{x0} has no effect. The I-type fields from high to low are the 12-bit immediate, five-bit source register, three-bit function field, five-bit destination register, and seven-bit opcode. For this form the function field is zero and the opcode is \texttt{0x13}. A selector accepting an IR constant must check that the 32-bit bit pattern is exactly the sign extension of its low twelve bits, rather than just masking the value down to fit.

For \verb|addi x5, x6, -1|, the immediate field is \texttt{0xFFF} and the instruction word is \texttt{0xFFF30293}. The little-endian instruction bytes are \texttt{93 02 F3 FF}. A test should decode those bytes, execute the resulting form with \texttt{x6} equal to zero, and expect \texttt{x5} to become \texttt{0xFFFFFFFF}. Other tests should accept $-2048$ and $2047$ and reject $-2049$ and $2048$ as signed immediate operands. For example, silently masking $2048$ would add $-2048$. Nonconstant operands or out-of-range constants need another pattern, such as materializing a constant and using register addition. This is a selection fallback, not a reason to extend the encoding range.

\section{Costs Have Units and Context}

\emph{The Marion System for Retargetable Instruction Scheduling} combines code selection and scheduling information in a machine description. A useful model distinguishes a result's latency from the number of issue opportunities an operation occupies and the execution resources it needs. This distinction matters even for a single opcode: several independent instances may be started before any result is ready. Conversely, two opcodes with interchangeable results can compete for the same execution resource. Intel's \emph{Optimization Reference Manual} treats latency, throughput, and model-specific behavior separately. A backend should record which core and form a scheduling entry models rather than treating a table entry as a property of the ISA.

Imagine a choice between one instruction that produces a result in four cycles and two dependent instructions that each take one cycle. The second sequence can shorten a critical path when resources are available. It may still lose in a block constrained by instruction fetch, issue capacity, or spills caused by extra live temporaries. A tree matcher can attach a cheap proxy to each rule, but a scheduler or late profitability check has more context. Rather than claiming one universal scalar, publish at least a size estimate and a named speed estimate, with an explicit policy for trading them off.

