\section{Schedule With Latency and Occupancy as Separate Fields}

Give a scheduler two numbers per instruction form: the
delay until its result is ready and the number of cycles
its issue resource remains occupied. Take a small acyclic
block already in a legal topological order. In this toy
machine each instruction needs one named resource, resources
can issue independently, and no other hazards exist. The
schedule preserves the given order on each resource while
allowing different resources to overlap. It is a feasible
greedy schedule, not an optimizer over all possible orders.

\begin{sudocode}
\Require Topologically ordered instructions $I$, timing model
\Let{F}{\operatorname{initialResourceTimes}(0)}
\ForAll{$i\in I$}
  \Let{r}{\operatorname{resource}(i)}
  \To{Sources must have completed before this issue.}
  \Let{k}{\operatorname{maxReadyTime}(\operatorname{inputs}(i))}
  \Let{s}{\max(k,F[r])}
  \State Place $i$ at cycle $s$
  \To{Completion and the next issue are different events.}
  \Let{\operatorname{ready}(i)}{s+\operatorname{latency}(i)}
  \Let{F[r]}{s+\operatorname{occupancy}(i)}
\EndFor
\State \Return issue times and result-ready times
\end{sudocode}

Give a pipelined multiply latency $4$ and occupancy $1$.
An add on a separate resource has both fields equal to $1$.
Two independent multiplies of initially ready inputs issue
at cycles $0$ and $1$, with results ready at $4$ and $5$.
Their consuming add issues at $5$ and finishes at $6$.
If occupancy is incorrectly set to $4$, the second multiply
issues at $4$ and the add finishes at $9$. Conversely,
two dependent multiplies cannot issue at $0$ and $1$:
the second needs the first result and must wait until $4$.
Keep both cases as tests of the description.

The invariant is that a placed instruction starts no sooner
than any source-ready time or the end of the resource interval
reserved by its previous occupant. The pass is linear in the
instruction and dependency counts, with inputs without local
producers ready at zero. Actual machines may have multiple
issue ports, shared bottlenecks, or operand-specific forwarding
latencies. This schedule is not a cycle bound on such a machine
unless the chosen description justifies those assumptions.

\section{Validate the Interfaces}

Exercise the encoding against an independent assembler or hand-derived vectors, not only against the generated decoder: two generated components can agree on the same wrong field layout. For execution, compare against an independent semantics or hardware where possible. For costs, use both dependent chains and independently executable forms, following Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-229}. Review learned corrections separately from instruction descriptions so a tuning update does not silently modify legality. The dossier's \href{https://github.com/Chubek/TheTomeOfLangDev/blob/HEAD/dossier-on-modern-cc-design/chapters/11-compiler-with-a-learned-cost-model.md}{Compiler with a Learned Cost Model} explores how such estimates can evolve without replacing the legality boundary.
